;
; SPI slave, Mode 0 only (CPOL=0, CPHA=0). MOSI is the IN pin, MISO is the
; OUT pin. SCK is read directly via an absolute `wait gpio` — not side-set,
; since side-set drives a pin and this slave does not drive SCK, the
; connected master does.
;
; CS is deliberately not synchronized here: this program reacts to SCK
; edges unconditionally. Correctness relies on the connected master
; never toggling SCK while this slave is deselected — standard SPI
; etiquette, and the same assumption satisfied by this project's own PIO
; SPI master (spi_master.pio), which only clocks SCK while its own CS is
; asserted. Firmware watches CS separately via plain GPIO, purely to count
; transaction boundaries — it does not gate this loop, and MISO is not
; tri-stated when deselected (fine for a single-slave bus).
;
; Same Mode-0 reasoning as the master, argued from what each
; instruction does rather than a memorized CPOL/CPHA table: MOSI is
; captured on the SCK rising edge, MISO is set up during the low phase
; beforehand.
;
; GPIO 12 is hardcoded below as the absolute SCK pin number, matching the
; frozen pin plan (hardware/pin_claims.yaml). `wait gpio N` takes an
; absolute pin index rather than a pin relative to this SM's own configured
; mapping, so — unlike the IN/OUT pins — it isn't parameterized from C.
.program spi_slave_mode0
.wrap_target
    wait 1 gpio 12  ; SCK rising edge: sample MOSI
    in  pins, 1
    wait 0 gpio 12  ; SCK falling edge: set up next MISO bit
    out pins, 1
.wrap
